<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etchmiadzin Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Mother Cathedral of Holy Etchmiadzin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etchmiadzin Cathedral, in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, is the mother church of the Armenian Apostolic Church and the seat of its Catholicos. Tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a> built it after a vision soon after Armenia adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the early 4th century, making it one of the oldest cathedrals in the world. The present building largely dates from a rebuilding in the late 5th century, with later additions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral.jpg" alt="Etchmiadzin Cathedral" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yerevantsi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etchmiadzin_2025-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
